"exercises": [
{"q": "How many boundary lines are needed in each direction to make the Chakra, and how many intervals do they create?", "a": "Ten lines in each direction create nine intervals in each direction."},
{"q": "Derive the number of cells and write the meaning of ekashitipada.", "a": "9 intervals x 9 intervals = 81 cells. Ekashitipada means 'having eighty-one positions' (eka, one, plus ashiti, eighty, plus pada, position)."},
{"q": "Complete the table: lines per direction 7, 11, 12. Give intervals and cells.", "a": "7 lines give 6 intervals and 36 cells. 11 lines give 10 intervals and 100 cells. 12 lines give 11 intervals and 121 cells. The formula is cells = (lines - 1) squared."},
{"q": "Name the four intermediate directions with their Sanskrit names.", "a": "North-East Ishanya, South-East Agneya, South-West Nairitya, North-West Vayavya."},
{"q": "Why must the grid be square and the intervals equal?", "a": "Equal rows and columns of equal size keep every cell comparable, give the structure symmetry, and make verification possible by measurement. Unequal cells make adjacency and distances ambiguous."},
{"q": "Convert R7-C2 to a single-number position, and convert position 63 back to a row and column.", "a": "R7-C2: (7 - 1) x 9 + 2 = 56. Position 63: 63 divided by 9 is exactly 7, so it is the last cell of row 7: R7-C9. (Check: (7 - 1) x 9 + 9 = 63.)"},
{"q": "A learner submits a sheet where all the cells look the same size but the two diagonals of the whole square measure 25.4 cm and 24.9 cm. What does this tell you and what should be done?", "a": "In a true square both diagonals are equal. A difference means the figure is slightly skewed, perhaps a parallelogram or rectangle. The learner should re-check the right angles and the side lengths and redraw the faulty lines. The test is a quick check for squareness."},
{"q": "Explain the difference between a cell's physical location and its assigned astrological identity. Why does the course keep them apart?", "a": "Physical location is where a square lies in the grid, given by row and column. Assigned identity is what the original diagram places there. Identity comes only from the source. Keeping them apart prevents the error of treating the illustrative grid, or a pattern of counts, as the authoritative positional diagram."},
{"q": "Evaluate: 'The centre cell R5-C5 must hold the most important element because it is the centre.' Is the reasoning valid?", "a": "No. The geometric centre is a fact of the grid. Whether the traditional Chakra assigns anything special there is not stated in the available passage and must be checked in the original diagram. The manual warns against confusing the geometric centre with a designated astrological position."},
{"q": "Design a verification record for a Chakra sheet with the nine checklist items, then explain how you would catch a mirror-image error that the checklist counts alone cannot reveal.", "a": "Record: ten horizontal lines; ten vertical lines; nine horizontal intervals; nine vertical intervals; 81 cells; equal dimensions (measured); four main directions marked; four intermediate directions marked; orientation preserved. Each gets pass or fail, date and signature. A mirror error leaves every count correct, so it is caught only by comparing the direction markers with the original diagram, preferably by laying a tracing of the original over the sheet, or by having another person check, and by marking one named reference corner on every sheet."}
],
