"exercises": [
{"q": "How many Swar entries should the working table hold, and how is that number reached from the number of Vidisha positions and rounds?", "a": "Sixteen. There are four Vidisha positions and four rounds, and 4 x 4 = 16. The manual states sixteen Swar over four rounds in four intermediate directions."},
{"q": "Name the first and last Nakshatras of the Chakra's sequence and say how many entries it has.", "a": "It begins at Krittika and ends with Bharani. It has 28 entries, because Abhijit is included between Uttarashadha and Shravana."},
{"q": "Using the serial numbers in module 3.2, which directional group does Hasta belong to, and what is its Chakra number?", "a": "Hasta is number 11, in the range Magha to Vishakha (8 to 14), the South group."},
{"q": "Convert general numbers to Chakra numbers: Krittika (3), Uttarashadha (21), Shravana (22), Revati (27).", "a": "Krittika 3 - 2 = 1. Uttarashadha 21 - 2 = 19. Shravana 22 - 1 = 21, because Abhijit (20) lies before it. Revati 27 - 1 = 26. Ashwini and Bharani, general 1 and 2, become 27 and 28."},
{"q": "A learner says: 28 Nakshatras divided into four groups gives seven cells per side, so I can list the 81 cells now. What is wrong?", "a": "The directional ranges are only a summary. The manual states that exact cell positions cannot be inferred by dividing the list into four groups, and the original diagram must be checked. The learner has treated a count as a map."},
{"q": "Longitude 72 degrees (HYPOTHETICAL). Give the Nakshatra, Pada, Rashi, Navamsha and the Chakra directional group.", "a": "72 / 13.333 = 5.4, so the sixth Nakshatra from Ashwini, Ardra (66 deg 40 min to 80 deg). Offset 72 - 66.667 = 5.333, which divided by 3.333 gives 1.6, so the second Pada. Sign: Mithuna (60 to 90), 12 degrees in, 12 / 3.333 = 3.6 so the fourth Navamsha. Ardra is number 4 in the Krittikadi list, so the East group."},
{"q": "Audit this transcribed list: Ma, Ta, Pa, Ra, Ta. Which entries do you flag and what do you write beside them?", "a": "Ta appears twice. Flag both and write 'confirm from original'. Possible reasons are two different letters merged by Latin spelling, or a copying slip. Do not delete, merge or rename either entry."},
{"q": "Explain the apparent conflict about Abhijit in module 3.2 and how you would record it without resolving it by guesswork.", "a": "The sequence puts Abhijit at number 20, inside the range Anuradha to Shravana, which the range table gives to West. A separate statement says the North group must include Abhijit. Moving Abhijit to North would make North eight and West six, breaking the seven-each statement. Record both statements with their locations, note the arithmetic, mark the entry 'confirm from original diagram', and use neither version as final."},
{"q": "Explain the two uses of Akara and why a Vedha rule needs to say which one is meant. Then say what you may and may not write on Day 3 about the effect of Vedha on the second Akara.", "a": "The first Akara is an independent Swar, one of the sixteen. The second Akara represents the specified five-Swar group (A, I, U, E, O). A rule about Akara could therefore affect one sound or a whole group, so the rule must say which. On Day 3 you may record that the distinction exists and that the effect is deferred. You may not state what the effect is, because the available text does not give it."},
{"q": "Longitude 278 degrees 50 minutes (HYPOTHETICAL). Name the Nakshatra and Pada in the 27-list, and explain how the 28-list may differ.", "a": "In the 27-list it is Uttarashadha, fourth Pada (276 deg 40 min to 280 deg). In the 28-list, with Abhijit commonly cited as about 276 deg 40 min to 280 deg 53 min 20 sec, the point falls in Abhijit. The span is cited as common but traditions differ, so the method and the span used must be recorded."}
],
"flash": [
["Swar", "A vowel sound, one that can be spoken by itself. The Chakra places sixteen Swar by its own prescribed list."],
["Varna", "A letter or phonetic unit. Placed in the Chakra in directional groups of five."],
["Vidisha", "An intermediate direction. The four Vidisha are Ishanya, Agneya, Nairitya and Vayavya."],
["Srishti Marg", "Literally the path of creation. In the manual's outline it is linked to clockwise progression of the Swar placement."],
["Ekashitipada", "Having eighty-one positions: the name of the Chakra."],
["Akaraadi", "Beginning with A. Also the root of the group names Akaradi, Ukaradi, Rikaradi and Okaradi."],
["Krittikadi order", "The Chakra's Nakshatra order, beginning at Krittika and ending at Bharani, with Abhijit included."],
["Abhijit", "The 28th Nakshatra of the Chakra, placed between Uttarashadha and Shravana."],
["Directional Nakshatra range", "East: Krittika to Ashlesha. South: Magha to Vishakha. West: Anuradha to Shravana. North: Dhanishtha to Bharani. Seven each."],
["Shatpada Chakra", "A Varna-to-Nakshatra table; Shatpada means six-footed. Linked in the manual to the Krittika start."],
["Five important Swar", "A, I, U, E and O."],
["Two Akaras", "The first is an independent Swar. The second represents the five-Swar group."],
["Pada and Navamsha (general)", "Both equal 3 degrees 20 minutes. 27 x 4 = 108 Padas and 12 x 9 = 108 Navamshas."],
["Source check", "A written note that an entry must be confirmed from the original diagram before use."]
],
"faq": [
["Why not just use the usual 27 Nakshatras?", "Because the Chakra is built on 28, with Abhijit included. The 28 divides into four groups of seven for the four directions. The manual tells you to keep the general list and the Chakra list apart."],
["Do I need to know Sanskrit to follow Day 3?", "You need to recognise the sounds and the letter names, not to read the verses. The key terms are explained in plain English. For the Sanskrit instruction on Swar placement, use the original text and ask a Sanskrit-trained reader if you are unsure."],
["Why does the lesson not give me the sixteen Swar in order?", "Because the order is not printed in the text available to the course and the manual forbids replacing it with a list of your own. Copy it from the original verse and diagram."],
["Is Abhijit a real star group or only a counting device?", "Abhijit is a traditional mansion linked with the bright star Vega in common accounts. Whether it is counted varies by tradition. In this Chakra it is counted, giving 28."],
["The Varna list has repeated letters. Should I fix it?", "No. Mark it and check the original. A repeat may be two letters that Latin spelling merges, or a slip. Fixing it silently would create a rule that is not in the source."],
["How long should it take to memorise the 28 Nakshatras?", "Most learners need several short sessions. Recite them in blocks of seven, one block per direction, and use the explorer tool. The goal is a correct, fluent order, not speed."],
["Will the Swar and Varna matter in practice, or are they only theory?", "They matter a great deal. Vedha is received by Nakshatra, Swar and Varna, and the Varnaadi Panchak on Day 10 is built from a name's first letter and its vowel."]
],
