"exercises": [
{"q": "Which three Rashis form the West group, and which the North group?", "a": "West: Vrischika, Dhanu, Makara. North: Kumbha, Meena, Mesha."},
{"q": "Which Rashi starts each of the four directional groups, and what do these four Rashis have in common in general Jyotish?", "a": "Vrishabha (East), Simha (South), Vrischika (West) and Kumbha (North). They are the four fixed (Sthira) signs. The manual does not state this as the reason for the grouping, so it is a memory aid only."},
{"q": "Give the category of Tithi numbers 7, 12 and 15.", "a": "7 is Bhadra (second class). 12 is Bhadra. 15 is Purna."},
{"q": "The Moon is 100 degrees ahead of the Sun (HYPOTHETICAL). Name the Tithi, Paksha and category.", "a": "100 / 12 = 8.33, so the 9th Tithi: Navami of Shukla Paksha. The category is Rikta."},
{"q": "The Moon is 200 degrees ahead of the Sun (HYPOTHETICAL). Name the Tithi, Paksha and category.", "a": "200 / 12 = 16.67, so the 17th Tithi of the month, which is Dwitiya of Krishna Paksha. The category is Bhadra."},
{"q": "Explain the difference between a Tithi and a Tithi category, and between 'Nanda Tithi' and 'Pratipada'.", "a": "A Tithi is a lunar day of 12 degrees of Moon-Sun distance. A category is a class into which Tithis are sorted. Nanda Tithi means any Tithi of the Nanda class (1, 6, 11 in a Paksha). Pratipada is the name of the first Tithi. Pratipada belongs to the Nanda class."},
{"q": "A student draws seven extra cells for the seven weekdays. Correct the error.", "a": "The manual states that separate cells are not assigned to Vara in the described arrangement. Weekdays are associated with the relevant Tithi groups. The student must remove the seven cells and record Vara references alongside the Tithi group, with the exact association copied from the original."},
{"q": "Explain the number 35 in connection with Tithi categories and weekdays, and say why the real pattern is not exactly 35 days.", "a": "The categories repeat every 5 Tithis and the weekdays every 7 days. The smallest common multiple is 35, so 35 pairings are possible if one Tithi passed per day. A real Tithi lasts about 19 to 26 hours, so Tithis do not match civil days and the cycle is not clean."},
{"q": "Run the counting check on the Chakra: 16 Swar, 28 Nakshatras, 12 Rashis, 5 Tithi positions, 20 Varnas. What is the total, what does it suggest, and why is it not a rule?", "a": "The total is 81, equal to the number of cells. It suggests every cell may hold one element, with Vara taking no cell. It is not a rule because the supplied text does not state this accounting, the Varna list is an uncertain transcription and the representative Akara might alter the count. Test it on the printed diagram and record the result."},
{"q": "At longitude 211 degrees (HYPOTHETICAL) the Rashi group is West and the Nakshatra group is South. What may you conclude and what may you not conclude?", "a": "You may record both groups and note the 3 degrees 20 minutes offset that produces the difference. You may not conclude that one group is correct, that the groups should agree, or that this shows a rule of the Chakra. Mark the point for confirmation from the source."}
],
