window.SBC.en.faq = [
["Is this a beginner course?", "No. The manual describes it as an intermediate-to-advanced, specialised programme. You should already know the nine Grahas, twelve Rashis, 27 Nakshatras with four Padas, Tithi, Vara, Paksha and retrograde motion. Use the Am I ready? check on the Start Here page."],
["Do I need to know Sanskrit?", "Basic familiarity with astrological terms helps, and you will meet verses with word meanings. You are not expected to be a Sanskrit scholar, but you are expected to check a rule against the original rather than rely on a paraphrase."],
["Does Vedha mean prediction?", "No. Vedha is a specific relationship identified under the prescribed Chakra rules. Identifying a Vedha does not automatically establish a price direction. The manual separates construction, Vedha identification and result interpretation, and each needs its own verification."],
["What is the difference between Drishti and Vedha?", "Drishti is the general aspect relationship under the aspect rules. Vedha is a Chakra-specific relationship under the Chakra's own rules. A planet can aspect something without satisfying any Vedha condition, so the course asks you to record the two separately."],
["Why does this Chakra use 28 Nakshatras?", "The manuscript includes Abhijit between Uttarashadha and Shravana, giving 28. The four directional groups then hold seven Nakshatras each. Do not confuse this with the familiar 27-Nakshatra system."],
["Why does the Nakshatra sequence start at Krittika?", "The Chakra uses its own special sequence, called Krittikadi, instead of the general order that starts at Ashwini. The manual links this to the Shatpada Chakra tradition and the Swar Shastra. Study the reasoning from the original and keep the two orders apart."],
["Why can't I use ordinary Shadbala?", "Swami Nirnaya in this course uses five prescribed Balas: Vakra, Udaya, Navamsha, Kshetra and Ucha. Each has its own reference point and proportional rule in the source. A generic strength calculation would replace those rules, which the manual specifically warns against."],
["Why are some tables left as 'confirm from the original'?", "The manuscript itself says some Varna groups and Nakshatra names are unclear, exact cell positions cannot be inferred from the directional lists, and some numerical expressions are not established by the available passage. The course does not invent anything to fill these gaps. This protects you from memorising an approximate rule."],
["Can I build the full Chakra from this app alone?", "You can learn the structure, counts, directional groups and procedures here. The cell-by-cell placement cannot be inferred from the summary lists, so the original Chakra diagram must be consulted and every entry verified against it."],
["How are Rahu and Ketu handled?", "Follow the specific rules in the source. Do not automatically apply the motion classification used for the other planets. Where the source rule is not in hand, mark it to confirm from the original."],
["Can I trade on this?", "No part of this course is investment or trading advice. It teaches a traditional method and how to test it, with no guaranteed outcome. Do not risk money you cannot afford to lose on the basis of any traditional indication."],
["What is a hypothesis log?", "It is the Market Validation Journal habit. Before you look at prices, you record the commodity, date, expected direction and period, and your reasons. Afterwards you compare with opening, closing, high and low prices and the percentage change, and you never edit the hypothesis."],
["How should I handle disagreements between schools or commentators?", "Keep the original rule and the commentary apart, and label the author's preferred reading as the author's. Where authorities differ, as with Madhyavedha or the corner Swar table, record the disagreement and your choice with reasons, and do not invent a number to settle it."],
["Do I need ephemeris software, and which ayanamsha?", "Yes, you need planetary positions, motion status and daily motion from an ephemeris. Use one consistent sidereal setting and write it down in each worksheet. The manual summary here does not fix an ayanamsha, so confirm what the original uses."],
["Can I finish faster or slower than 14 days?", "Yes. The course is self-paced: 14 days of about 120 minutes, or 28 half-days of about 60 minutes. Do not advance to a calculation until you understand the preceding stage."],
["Will this make me able to forecast prices?", "The aim is research discipline: a documented, rule-based study that you can test against actual price data. Some hypotheses will fail or be inconclusive, and the course asks you to record those honestly."]
]
;
window.SBC.en.day0 = {
"title": "Start Here: How to Use This Course",
"blocks": [
["h3", "Who this course is for"],
["p", "Sarvatobhadra Chakra Basics in Market Astrology is a 14-day programme by Anoopam Shukla (ASTROSUTRAS BY ANOOPAM). It teaches Arghakand, the traditional study of commodity value, through the 81-cell Sarvatobhadra Chakra. It suits practising Vedic astrologers, students of the Chakra, researchers of traditional Vedha systems, and market analysts who want to examine a traditional framework with discipline."],
["box", "The word 'Basics' refers to the Chakra's basics, not to a beginner's level. The manual calls this an intermediate-to-advanced, specialised programme. It is not an introduction to Vedic astrology."],
["h3", "Honest prerequisites"],
["p", "Before Day 1 you should already be comfortable with:"],
["li", "The nine Grahas, including Rahu and Ketu."],
["li", "The twelve Rashis."],
["li", "The 27 Nakshatras and their four Padas each."],
["li", "Tithi, Vara and Paksha."],
["li", "Planetary positions and the idea of retrogression."],
["p", "Helpful but not mandatory: Navamsha, some Sanskrit astrological terms, ratios and proportion, and a basic familiarity with price charts."],
["h3", "Am I ready? A 20-minute self-check"],
["p", "Answer each question yes or no, honestly, without notes. Spend no more than 20 minutes in total."],
["ol", "Can I name the nine Grahas and say which two are shadow points?|Can I list the twelve Rashis in zodiac order?|Can I recite the 27 Nakshatras in order from Ashwini and say that each has four Padas?|Can I say what a Tithi is, tell Shukla from Krishna Paksha, and name the seven weekdays?|Can I explain retrograde motion as an apparent effect and tell from an ephemeris whether a planet is retrograde?|Can I turn a sidereal longitude into a Rashi, Nakshatra and Pada (a Nakshatra spans 13 degrees 20 minutes, a Pada 3 degrees 20 minutes)?|Can I solve a simple proportion such as A/B = C/X for X?|Can I read a price chart: opening, closing, high, low and percentage change?"],
["p", "Seven or eight yes answers: you are ready. Five or six: revise the weak points first, then start. Fewer than five: take a foundation course in Vedic astrology before this one. That is a sound decision, not a failure."],
["h3", "How a 120-minute day is organised"],
["tbl", "Minutes|Part|What you do;;10|Recap|Recall the previous day without looking;;40|Concept|Read the lesson and its rules, with the original reference;;25|Worked example|Follow a step-by-step illustration;;35|Practical|Fill in the worksheet yourself;;10|Check|Answer the knowledge-check cards"],
["p", "Fourteen days of 120 minutes make 28 hours of guided study. Do not skip the recap, because each day builds on the last."],
["h3", "Using the worksheets"],
["p", "Each day has a worksheet. Fill it in as you work, not afterwards. Write the source reference for every entry. If you cannot support an entry from the manual or the original diagram, write 'confirm from the original' instead of guessing. A worksheet with honest gaps is worth more than a complete one with invented values."],
["h3", "The Market Validation Journal"],
["p", "One journal runs across all fourteen days. The habit is simple: record your hypothesis before you look at the outcome. For each entry note the commodity, the date, the expected direction and period, and your reasons. Only afterwards compare it with the actual opening, closing, high and low prices and the percentage change. Never edit the hypothesis after the result is known. Record inconclusive and contradictory outcomes as well."],
["h3", "The four-level rule format"],
["p", "Every important rule is presented in four levels:"],
["ol", "Original textual reference: the Sanskrit verse, prose instruction or table|Traditional interpretation: its literal and commentarial meaning|Academic explanation: the reasoning, calculation and application in modern language|Practical application and limitations: a demonstration, its limits and any unresolved question"],
["h3", "Source Safeguard boxes"],
["p", "Each day carries a Source Safeguard box. It marks what must be confirmed from the original diagram or verse. The manual itself names several areas needing verification: unclear Varna groups and Nakshatra names, cell-by-cell placement that cannot be inferred from the directional lists, differing opinions on the corner Swar, the numerical expressions in the Drishti Pada verse, and the exact strength calculations."],
["box", "These boxes exist so that you never memorise an invented or approximate rule. Treat them as research safeguards, not as weaknesses in the course."],
["h3", "Study pace"],
["li", "Standard pace: one day of about 120 minutes, for 14 consecutive days."],
["li", "Relaxed pace: 28 half-days of about 60 minutes, splitting each day into two sittings."],
["li", "Whatever the pace, do not advance to a calculation until you understand the stage before it."],
["h3", "Ethics and disclaimer"],
["box", "This programme is for education and research. It teaches a traditional method and how to test it. It is not investment, trading or financial advice, and no outcome is guaranteed. A Vedha does not establish a price direction by itself. Do not risk money you cannot afford to lose on the basis of any traditional indication."],
["h3", "Recording unresolved questions"],
["p", "You will meet points that the manual leaves open. Do not smooth them over. In your journal keep a short list with four items for each:"],
["ol", "The question in one sentence|Where it arose (day, rule, worksheet)|What the manual says and what it leaves undecided|What you will check in the original diagram or verse"],
["p", "Return to this list at the end of the course. A well-kept list of open questions is part of the research discipline the course aims to build."]
]
}
;
